# Both unknown blocks below have the exact shape of a require_broker entry
# (`- op_pattern:` + `primitive:`). The parser leaves each pair PENDING and
# flushes it when the next top-level key arrives — and there are TWO distinct
# flush sites, one per top-level-key rule:
#
#   `vendor_hints`             -> flushed by a top-level SCALAR key
#                                 (`broker_min_version:` below)
#   `broker_capabilities_seen` -> flushed by a top-level LIST OPENER key
#                                 (`require_broker:` below)
#
# A flush that hardcodes the label `require_broker` files the bogus pair into
# the lookup table AHEAD of the real entries, so `lookup_primitive` returns the
# first bogus match and the remediation of a genuine `git push` block silently
# changes. Each block targets a different primitive, so the fixture names which
# site regressed: `kvendra.shell` = scalar site, `kvendra.http` = list-opener
# site, `kvendra.git` = both correct. Each flush must use the pair's own parent
# key, exactly as the END block already does.
